damage: survive teardown — clear the screen-private slot and guard Unregister

Exiting the desktop session crashed Xphoenix-glamor-daemon every time.
Two mechanisms, one cause: damage's screen private outliving nothing.

damageCloseScreen() freed pScrPriv and then called the downstream
CloseScreen, leaving a DANGLING POINTER in the private slot. glamor
destroys its GCs during that downstream chain, and
glamor_invalidate_stipple() (glamor/glamor_core.c) calls DamageUnregister,
which does damageScrPriv() and then an INDIRECT CALL through
pScrPriv->funcs.Unregister. On this board that showed up first as a Data
Abort reading 0x60 (the privates address handed to dixGetPrivate was
&((base)NULL)->devPrivates) and, once the free was merely deferred, as a
PC alignment fault at 0xaa1403e0aa1503e2 -- a jump through the stale
function pointer, the value being two AArch64 instruction words.

Deferring the free alone is not enough, because a lookup on a freed-but-
still-populated slot returns garbage rather than NULL and nothing
downstream can tell the difference. So also clear the slot after freeing,
which converts the use-after-free into a NULL that callers can check, and
check it in DamageUnregister before the indirect call.

Also guard DamageUnregister against a NULL pDrawable: it clears pDrawable
on its way out, and glamor reaches it twice on GC teardown -- once from
glamor_invalidate_stipple and once from the DamageDestroy callback -- so
the second call dereferenced NULL.

The Unregister call is scoped so pScrPriv does not outlive its check; the
rest of the function does not use it (getDrawableDamageRef declares its
own, and is unreachable once we return early).

--- a/miext/damage/damage.c
+++ b/miext/damage/damage.c
@@ -1595,14 +1595,31 @@
 static Bool
 damageCloseScreen(ScreenPtr pScreen)
 {
+    Bool ret;
+
     damageScrPriv(pScreen);
 
     unwrap(pScrPriv, pScreen, DestroyPixmap);
     unwrap(pScrPriv, pScreen, CreateGC);
     unwrap(pScrPriv, pScreen, CopyWindow);
     unwrap(pScrPriv, pScreen, CloseScreen);
+
+    /* Free the screen private only after the downstream CloseScreen returns, and
+     * then CLEAR THE PRIVATE SLOT. Layers wrapped below damage still reach into
+     * it while they close -- glamor destroys its GCs, and glamor_invalidate_stipple
+     * (glamor/glamor_core.c) calls DamageUnregister, which does damageScrPriv()
+     * and then an INDIRECT CALL through pScrPriv->funcs.Unregister.
+     *
+     * Leaving a dangling pointer in the slot is what made that unrecoverable: a
+     * later lookup returned freed memory rather than NULL, so no amount of
+     * checking downstream could tell the difference, and the indirect call jumped
+     * through whatever the allocator had put there. Clearing the slot turns a
+     * use-after-free into a NULL the guard in DamageUnregister can act on. */
+    ret = (*pScreen->CloseScreen) (pScreen);
     free(pScrPriv);
-    return (*pScreen->CloseScreen) (pScreen);
+    dixSetPrivate(&pScreen->devPrivates, damageScrPrivateKey, NULL);
+
+    return ret;
 }
 
 /**
@@ -1791,11 +1808,28 @@
 DamageUnregister(DamagePtr pDamage)
 {
     DrawablePtr pDrawable = pDamage->pDrawable;
-    ScreenPtr pScreen = pDrawable->pScreen;
+    ScreenPtr pScreen;
 
-    damageScrPriv(pScreen);
+    /* Already unregistered: DamageUnregister clears pDrawable on its way out, so
+     * a second call (glamor reaches here twice on GC teardown -- once from
+     * glamor_invalidate_stipple, once from the DamageDestroy callback) would
+     * dereference NULL. */
+    if (pDrawable == NULL)
+        return;
+
+    pScreen = pDrawable->pScreen;
+
+    {
+        damageScrPriv(pScreen);
+
+        /* Screen already torn down (damageCloseScreen ran and cleared the slot).
+         * Nothing left to unregister from, and funcs.Unregister below is an
+         * indirect call that would jump through freed memory. */
+        if (pScrPriv == NULL)
+            return;
 
-    (*pScrPriv->funcs.Unregister) (pDrawable, pDamage);
+        (*pScrPriv->funcs.Unregister) (pDrawable, pDamage);
+    }
 
     if (pDrawable->type == DRAWABLE_WINDOW) {
         WindowPtr pWindow = (WindowPtr) pDrawable;
